**Banana-blocks** are also known as banana-brackets, B-brackets, or just "a banana".  This lisp'y little construct is the most important primitive in the CMK language.  It comes in different forms, but before we get deep into the grammar, here's a simple example.

You can put any kind of text inside, but here's what a banana emoji inside a banana block would look like:

{{ cmkmath.display("(| .. 🍌 🍌 🍌 .. |)", class="centered") }}

This page builds from the simple variations into more complicated use gradually, but let's get the rough definitions out of the way.  Banana-block syntax *generalizes quotes*[^qq] by adding pre/post processors and constructors into the mix.  Additionally, they are deeply connected with the CMK compiler / interpreter / runtime in various ways, which makes them a useful handle on CMK's facilities for [extensibility and metaprogramming]({{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming).

{{ macros.section(3, "Quoted Bananas", id="banana-quoted") }}

A banana-block is usually spanning many lines. That's part of the point, but we'll write them as one line just to keep it simple.  Since they hold anything you can just embed data or code like this:

{{ cmkmath.algebra([
  ["@json_data",     "(|.. many lines of JSON ..|)"],
  ["@polyglot_code", "(|.. many lines of foreign language ..|)"],
], arrow="=") }}

There's an equivalent, more declarative version of simple assignment as well: just leave out the equals sign:

{{ cmkmath.display("@json_data (|.. many lines of JSON ..|)") }}

{# ########################################################## #}

{{ macros.section(3, "Quasi Quoted Bananas", id="banana-quasiquote") }}

A quasi-quote is "data with holes in it": what you might otherwise call a template, or a macro.  Since a banana is completely lazy about its interior, that's fair game too; a block can carry holes and fill them in later.

The holes are ordinary `${..}` references.  A banana freezes its body *verbatim*, so while the block just sits there quoted the holes stay literal, part of the inert source: <!-- docs-code-ok -->

{{ cmkmath.display("@greeting = (| Hello, ${who}! |)") }}

Nothing is expanded yet: `@greeting`'s stored value is the exact text `Hello, ${who}!`, hole and all.  The hole is filled later, when the block is *used*: referencing `@greeting` in a scope that binds `who` interpolates it in place, so a single template yields a different result per scope. <!-- docs-code-ok -->

{{ cmkmath.algebra([
  ["@who = alice", "@greeting is `Hello, alice!`"],
  ["@who = bob",   "@greeting is `Hello, bob!`"],
]) }}

That gap, between a block that carries `${who}` verbatim and a scope that fills it, is the whole point of a quasi-quote.  Filling a bare hole like this is just the surrounding language's ordinary expansion.  The *deeper* unquote is **cooking**: a second compiler pass that additionally wakes up the block's cmk-lang interior (the [callforms](#banana-channels), target-anchored dispatches, and nested bananas that plain expansion leaves untouched) and lowers them in place.  That *quote vs cook* distinction is what the [squashed](#banana-squashed) and [cooked](#banana-cooked) forms below are built on (more on this later). <!-- docs-code-ok -->

The choice of *treatment* is spelled by the **assignment operator**.  Same body, three fates, decided at compile time:

{{ cmkmath.algebra([
  ["@block =  (| .. |)", "quoted: kept verbatim"],
  ["@block := (| .. |)", "cooked: interior lowered"],
  ["@block <- (| .. |)", "run once, result captured"],
]) }}

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-assign.cmk', github=github) }}

Filling the holes is its own operator, too.  Because a quoted banana is *just a string*, it carries a small **string algebra**: the `%` operator fills a block's `@@holes@@` from a second (kwargs) banana, and since every result is another banana, `%` chains left-to-right.  This is the explicit, banana-level twin of the ambient `${..}` expansion above, a template-fill you can drive by hand: <!-- docs-code-ok -->

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-templates.cmk', github=github) }}

So far, if you're thinking *only* about multiline data, then bananas might appear similar to the [triple-quoted forms]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#triple-quote-literals) or [block-reference syntax]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#block-references).

Those are useful primitives too, but no comparison!  Because of *construction, composition, and the [callform algebra]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#callforms)* they can use.. we'll see that bananas are on a completely different level.

{# ########################################################## #}

{{ macros.section(3, "Squashed Bananas", id="banana-squashed") }}

Squashed bananas are like banana-blocks, but the interior is **compiled**.  A mnemonic for the syntax is that square-bracket brutalism has squashed the elegant curve out of the parens in the last section.

{{ cmkmath.display("@program = [|.. many lines of CMK-Lang ..|]") }}

If you started with an *unsquashed* banana, you can get the reduction from walrus-assignment.

{{ cmkmath.display("@squashed := @unsquashed (|..|)") }}

By default squashing is a **deep cooker** that accomodates nested bananas and nested versions of the other [basic forms]({{_}}/cmk/quickref/#banana-forms).  This is really an instance of a more general pattern we'll come around to, but this form comes up so often that it gets special syntax.  As an aside: terms like "squashed", "cooked", and "reduced" refer to *lowering* in the context of the compiler, but [details about that]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#multistage) aren't needed for the high-level operations we'll discuss here.

Anyway, you might look at this and think, **why bother?**  Unlike a multiline [polyglot]({{_}}/demos/polyglots), a *cmk program* would be legal syntax *outside* the wrapper, right?  So maybe you'd only wrap it to transport code you don't want to execute?  

That's not too far from the truth. Getting sneaky with closures[^closure] *is* fundamentally why banana-blocks can bootstrap into [object-oriented programming](#banana-cooked) and [actors]({{_}}/stages/#channels-agents).  But it's also the basis of the [module system]({{_}}/cmk/modules), and why you can [run CMK *from* the ur-language underneath it]({{_}}/cmk/paradigm/#interpreters-transpilation), and how CMK [is moving towards self-hosted]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#multistage).

{# ########################################################## #}

{{ macros.section(3, "Peeled Bananas", id="banana-peeled") }}

Peeled bananas look like squashed ones, but they don't appear at module level, and they are **actually executed** at runtime.  Thus they appear inside a target/function body:

```Makefile
target:
    [| .. many lines of CMK-Lang .. |]
```

The demo below builds callable objects from banana-forms and invokes them from inside recipe bodies, where they run at runtime:

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-recipes.cmk', github=github) }}


{# `NAME(| body |)`: a native, generic *declare*.  It keeps a body (a script, a Dockerfile, a jq program, a class body) in source, then hands it to a **constructor** that acts on it.  That makes the banana the block-structured sibling of the [triple-quoted forms]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#triple-quote-literals) and [block-references]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#block-references); a way to name a snippet and then *do something* with it. #}

{# {{ macros.section(3, "Quote, Compile, Eval", id="banana-basics") }}

Before the full family, three **degenerate cases** carry most of the intuition; they line up with a trio you already know, **quote** / **compile** / **eval**:

* **Quote**: `x = (| body |)` captures the block as *data*.  `x` names the raw body, inert and exactly as written; like quoting code instead of running it.
* **Compile**: `x := (| body |)` (equivalently `x = [| body |]`) *cooks* the block: the cmk-lang inside it (`this.X(..)`, the [call-forms](#banana-channels), `${..}`) wakes up and is compiled, and `x` names the result.
* **Eval**: inside a recipe, a bare `[| body |]` cooks the block **and runs it, right there**.

It is the same `[| .. |]` bracket in the last two: *assigned* at module level it yields a compiled value; *bare* in a recipe it runs.  The only thing that varies across all three is **when the body wakes up**: never, at compile time, or on the spot.

Everything below generalizes these: other constructors, extra bodies, the `[stream]` / `(args)` / `{env}` callform-channels, and the postfix treatments that decide cooking.  Those callform-channels share the grammar documented under [Call Forms]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#callforms); here we cover what is banana-specific and link out for the rest. #}

{% include "includes/banana-forms.md" %}

