{{ macros.section(3, "The Block", id="banana-block") }}

`NAME(| body |)` names a block, then hands it to a **constructor** named by a **leading path**:

| form | constructor |
|---|---|
| leading dotpath `code.unbound X(| .. |)` | `code.unbound` |
| leading words `code unbound X(| .. |)` | same, spelled as words |
| reader-intent alias `code.config X(| .. |)` | `code.config` (== `code.unbound`, for data/config blobs) |
| `using k=v` | order-free kwargs passed to the constructor |

The delimiter is **Idris idiom-safe**: a bare `(| .. |)` *inside* a body passes through untouched, and the multi-line close is `|)` **alone on its line**.  Like every CMK construct, the block is inert inside `define`-blocks and comments.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-blocks.cmk', github=github) }}
#}

{{ macros.section(3, "As Callables", id="banana-channels") }}

A banana is also a **callable**: its three **callform-channels** (the block-trailers; distinct from the event-bus `channel` feature) turn a block into something you apply, filter, or configure.  A block takes at most **one** *block-trailer* (they are alternatives, not a stack).  These are exactly the same channels that ride any name, the [callform algebra](#banana-channels):

{% include "includes/callform-algebra.md" %}

The env, args, and stream brackets are lexically distinct from one another (and from an ordinary variable reference), so all three can ride the same block at once without ambiguity.  A block can likewise be captured into a variable while still carrying a stream or args channel.  (For how each channel is compiled, see the compiler's [Call Forms]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#callforms).)

