{{ macros.section(3, "Turbo Banana", id="banana-multibody") }}

The **turbo banana** is **multi-body currying**: {{ cmkmath.imath("[@ctor] @NAME (|@A|)(|@B|)..") }} hands **N block bodies** to a single constructor; the first body is the primary, and each extra is an additional body the constructor can pick up.  `(|` vs `(` is what tells a second block from an `(args)` trailer, so the two never collide.  This needs a constructor that understands multiple bodies (see [Constructors](#banana-declare)).

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-composition.cmk', github=github) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Lambda-lift", id="banana-lambda") }}

An **anonymous** block in a recipe is the one **recipe**-level banana (every other form is module-level).  With no name to bind, it runs right where it sits: the block is lifted[^lambdalift] to a hidden module define and dispatched into an **ambient** by the `in` trailer: `(| body |) in <ambient>` runs it on a host runner, inside a container, or in a service, with env and args riding the `{}`/`()` [callform-channels](#banana-channels) above.  A **typed machine constructor** gives the same, more explicitly: `host.native.sh(| body |){WHO=world}` runs a shell snippet with the env forwarded (the demo below uses exactly this, so it needs no docker).  A *bare* anonymous `(| body |){env}(args)` with no `in` and no constructor is deliberately **reserved**: an anonymous banana has only a string algebra, so a `{env}`/`(args)` trailer needs a typed machine to give it a call-form.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-lambda.cmk', github=github) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Fluent Bananas", id="banana-fluent") }}

An **operator between two banana constructions**, {{ cmkmath.imath("@foo (|@a|) . @bar (|@b|)") }}, is a **binary operator** over blocks: the sibling of the `__call__` invoke.  At a recipe each operand is constructed as a throwaway instance (lifted to module scope like a [lambda](#banana-lambda)), then folded left-to-right through each operand's **dunder** for that operator, with the final result invoked inline.  So a longer chain constructs each block, folds them pairwise through the operator's dunder, and runs once at the end.

The operator character picks the dunder from a small **catalog**, and a kind implements only the ones it wants:

{{ cmkmath.algebra([
  ["`.`", "`__dot__`"],
  ["`/`", "`__div__`"],
  ["`|`", "`__pipe__`"],
]) }}

Because the meaning lives in that dunder, the **same chain syntax composes differently per kind**.  In the demo, the `seq` kind implements the whole catalog (`.` sequences, `/` and-thens, `|` pipes), while its `pipe` kind overrides only the dot so that `.` *means* pipe.  Each operator handler folds the right-hand block into an accumulator and returns itself so the chain continues, and the final invoke runs the accumulated command.  Operands may be single-line (lifted by [lambda-lift](#banana-lambda)) or multiline (opened at end of line, closed by a leading `|)`), and this same operator is what the bash control-flow demos (`if` / `then` / `else`, and the loops) are built from.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-fluent.cmk', github=github) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Squashed Bananas", id="banana-cooked") }}

A block body is itself cmk-lang, but by default a **raw** block is kept *verbatim*, as inert data; the language constructs inside it are not active.  **Cooking** turns them back on, so the same body's interior evaluates in place.  A raw block is data; a cooked block is code:

{% set cook_raw %}
# kept verbatim: the callform and ${..} are just text
(| greet:; cmk.log(hi ${who}) |)
{% endset %}
{% set cook_cooked %}
# interior woken up, lowered in place: cmk.log(..)
# fires, and this.X(..) / ${..} are all live
[| greet:; cmk.log(hi ${who}) |]
{% endset %}
{{ site.inline_mirror('mirror_banana_cook', cook_raw, cook_cooked,
    label_left='Raw (| .. |)', type_left='cmk', lang_left='cmk',
    label_right='Cooked [| .. |]', default='left') }}

Cooking is the most common member of a broader mechanism: **postfix treatments**.  After the close `|)` a block takes a comma-separated *list* of treatments, applied left-to-right at compile time (the trailer grammar lives on the [Quick Reference]({{_}}/cmk/quickref/#banana-forms)).  Two are **builtin**: `cooked` (lower the interior) and `cooked_deeply` (also cook any *nested* banana); any *other* word is a **stdlib target** the body is piped through at compile time, so the set is open and extensible (a missing/failing target is a compile error).

You rarely write the words.  A bracket family stands in for the common treatments, and the assignment operator picks raw or cooked implicitly:

{{ cmkmath.algebra([
  ["(| .. |)", "raw: kept verbatim, inert data"],
  ["[| .. |]", "the common `cooked_deeply` case, usable everywhere `(| .. |)` is"],
  ["{| .. |}", "whatever treatment the `block_brackets` pragma assigns"],
  [":= vs = / <-", "`:=` cooks; `=` and `<-` stay raw"],
]) }}

The demo below puts that open slot to work: its `block_brackets` pragma teaches the `{| .. |}` bracket the `stream.strip` treatment, so each brace-bracket body is normalized to one clean line at compile time, while a raw `(| .. |)` keeps its spacing verbatim.  Any stdlib target that reads stdin and writes stdout can serve as the treatment.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-user-defined.cmk', github=github) }}

Cooking is one instance of CMK's broader **staging** story (turning a block back from data into code), and it composes with the run-time re-compilation primitives (`lang.transpile`, `native_target`) behind hosted and vanilla-makefile builds.  For that whole picture, see the compiler's [Multistage]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#multistage) appendix.

The `banana-oop` demo builds a small object system from **cooked class bodies**: each `class NAME[| .. |]` body is cooked (the `[|` bracket is `cooked_deeply`), so its instance-keyed method targets and their target-anchored and expansion interiors are live.  Calling `Class` with a name binds the instance handle and stamps the class's members onto that instance.  A subclass names its parent among its bases to inherit the whole chain (`Dog is-a Animal`) and overrides a method just by redefining it; when two bases define the same method the **leftmost wins**, exactly like Python's MRO.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-oop.cmk', github=github) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "Constructors", id="banana-declare") }}

The constructor a block is handed to is an ordinary [`declare.*`]({{_}}/macros/#declare-namespace) macro, so you can define your own.  The constructor is named by the block's **leading path**: `declare docker lambda NAME(| .. |)` hands the block to `declare.docker.lambda`.  The demo below defines `declare.docker.lambda` (scaffold a build+run target from an inlined Dockerfile body) and drives it with a banana.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-callables.cmk', github=github) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "As Namespaces", id="banana-namespace") }}

The built-in **`namespace`** constructor is the banana form of [a namespace]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#namespaces): a squashed banana whose every declared target and variable is prefixed with the namespace's name.  (`namespace` is a [`cmk` prelude]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#prelude) keyword: bare after `from cmk import *`, else `cmk.namespace`.)

{{ cmkmath.algebra([
  ["#namespace @tier1 [|foo:; echo hi|]", "a target `tier1.foo`"],
]) }}

Nesting **composes** the names, so the bodies stay flat and readable while the targets come out fully-qualified:

{{ cmkmath.algebra([
  ["#namespace @tier1 [|#namespace @tier2 [|foo:; echo hi|]|]", "`tier1.tier2.foo`"],
]) }}

Each level threads its name through the `__name__` variable and the inner banana closes over it (the same closure trick squashing is built on), and every instance records its own path as a fully-qualified `tier1.tier2.__name__`.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/ambients-open.cmk', github=github) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "As Modules", id="banana-module") }}

Where a namespace just prefixes an inline body, a **module** is a namespace with a *boundary*: a first-class ambient you either **mount** (nested, reached qualified) or **dissolve** (flat, reached bare).  Sourced from a file the split is `import M as ns` versus `open M`, both mounting through `import.module` -- the [modules page]({{_}}/cmk/modules) and its [Module Algebra]({{_}}/cmk/modules/#module-algebra) card carry every disk-side keyword.  Constructed *inline*, the same split is two banana forms:

{{ cmkmath.algebra([
  ["#module @tools [| .. |]", "mount an inline module under `tools.*`"],
  ["*[| .. |]",               "dissolve a block flat into scope"],
]) }}

The dissolve form is the **ambient-explode** technique compose.mk uses in its own [`__hosted__`]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#multistage) region: author a whole family of targets in one block at a shallow indent, then explode them flat in place.  The demo mounts an inline module and explodes a block side by side:

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-module.cmk', github=github) }}

{{ macros.section(3, "As Machine", id="banana-machine") }}

Every "As X" above is one KIND: a [**machine**]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#machines), any run context a banana can execute in.  The built-in machines need no declaration -- the host interpreters `sh`, `bash`, `python`, plus `out` -- and a `container` is just a machine created with `using bases=cmk.machine` that dispatches into docker.  In banana form:

{{ cmkmath.algebra([
  ["(|pip list|) #in #bash",    "a built-in host machine (no docker)"],
  ["(|..|) #in #sh",            "the same shape, run by the #sh interpreter"],
  ["#container @box (|img=..|)", "a machine backed by an image"],
]) }}

Every machine grows a per-instance dispatch target that routes a block through its `.run` hook (a host command dispatch by default), so `in <machine>` and the bare `NAME[block]` callform reach the same dispatch.  New subkinds are created exactly the way `container` is: a [`cmk.class`](#banana-declare) `using bases=cmk.machine` that overrides `.entrypoint` / `.run`.  The operator grid for all of these run-side forms is on the [quickref]({{_}}/cmk/quickref/#banana-forms).

{{ macros.section(3, "As Ambient", id="banana-ambient") }}

A recipe-level banana {{ cmkmath.imath("(| .. |)") }} is a raw string until an *ambient* operator (`in` / `out` / `open`) attaches it to a **machine**; those operators move it between execution contexts under a *mobile-ambient calculus*[^ambient] over `make` (concatenation and the typed pipe are composition operators, not ambient moves; both are covered by the [banana algebra](#banana-algebra) card above).  The [operator algebra and register model]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#ambients) live on the concepts page; here it is in running code.  Two banana-specific niceties worth flagging: `out` from inside a container escapes to the host daemon over the mounted socket (the calculus's *unforgeable exit capability*), and under [`<-`]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#capture-operator) the pipeline's final stdout is captured.

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/ambients.cmk', github=github) }}

